      "Here's the solution with step-by-step comments:\n\n```python\nfrom operator import itemgetter \n\ndef index_minimum(test_list):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a list of tuples, returns the first value of the tuple with the smallest second value.\n\n    Example Outputs:\n    index_minimum([('Rash', 143), ('Manjeet', 200), ('Varsha', 100)]) == 'Varsha'\n    index_minimum([('Yash', 185), ('Dawood', 125), ('Sanya', 175)]) == 'Dawood'\n    index_minimum([('Sai', 345), ('Salman', 145), ('Ayesha', 96)]) == 'Ayesha'\n    \"\"\"\n    # Use the `itemgetter` function from the `operator` module to extract the second element (index 1) of each tuple\n    # This allows us to sort the list of tuples based on the second element (the smaller value)\n    sorted_list = sorted(test_list, key=itemgetter(1))\n    \n    # The tuple with the smallest second value is now the first element in the sorted list\n    # So we return the first element (index 0) of that tuple\n    return sorted_list[0][0]\n```\n\nHere's how the code works:\n\n1. We import the `itemgetter` function from the `operator` module, which will be used to sort the list of tuples based on the second element of each tuple.\n2. The `index_minimum` function takes a list of tuples as input.\n3. Inside the function, we use the `sorted` function to sort the list of tuples based on the second element of each tuple (the smaller value). We do this by passing the `itemgetter(1)` function to the `key` parameter of `sorted`. This tells the `sorted` function to use the second element (index 1) of each tuple as the basis for sorting.\n4. After sorting the list, the tuple with the smallest second value is now the first element in the sorted list. We return the first element (index 0) of that tuple, which is the first value of the tuple with the smallest second value.\n\nThe example outputs provided in the starter code are used to test the function and ensure it's working as expected.",
