      'Here\'s the solution to the problem:\n\n```python\ndef tuple_to_dict(test_tup):\n    """\n    Converts a given tuple to a key-value dictionary using adjacent elements.\n\n    Args:\n        test_tup (tuple): The input tuple to be converted.\n\n    Returns:\n        dict: A dictionary where the keys are the odd-indexed elements and the values are the even-indexed elements of the input tuple.\n    """\n    # Initialize an empty dictionary to store the key-value pairs\n    result = {}\n\n    # Iterate through the tuple in steps of 2, using the odd-indexed elements as keys and the even-indexed elements as values\n    for i in range(0, len(test_tup), 2):\n        result[test_tup[i]] = test_tup[i+1]\n\n    return result\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n1. The function `tuple_to_dict` takes a tuple `test_tup` as input.\n2. We initialize an empty dictionary `result` to store the key-value pairs.\n3. We iterate through the tuple `test_tup` in steps of 2, using the `range` function. This allows us to access the odd-indexed elements (the keys) and the even-indexed elements (the values).\n4. For each iteration, we add the key-value pair to the `result` dictionary, where the key is the odd-indexed element and the value is the even-indexed element.\n5. Finally, we return the `result` dictionary.\n\nThis function will convert the given tuple to a key-value dictionary using adjacent elements, as shown in the example outputs provided in the starter code.',
