      'Here is an example code for a Steam bot that randomly selects users and sends them 10 trading cards:\n```python\nimport steam\nimport random\n\n# Set up your Steam API credentials\nsteam_api_key = "YOUR_STEAM_API_KEY"\nsteam_api_secret = "YOUR_STEAM_API_SECRET"\nsteam_api_app_id = 440  # Steam Trading app ID\n\n# Set up the bot\'s Steam profile\nbot_steam_id = "YOUR_STEAM_ID"\n\n# Set up the list of users to trade with\nusers_to_trade_with = []\n\n# Connect to the Steam API\nsteam_api = steam.SteamAPI(steam_api_key, steam_api_secret)\n\n# Get a list of all Steam users\nusers = steam_api.GetUsers()\n\n# Loop through each user and add them to the list of users to trade with\nfor user in users:\n    users_to_trade_with.append(user.steamid)\n\n# Set up the bot\'s trading options\nbot_trading_card_limit = 10\nbot_trading_card_type = steam.TradingCardType.Any\n\n# Main loop\nwhile True:\n    # Randomly select a user to trade with\n    user_to_trade_with = random.choice(users_to_trade_with)\n\n    # Get the user\'s Steam profile\n    user_profile = steam_api.GetProfile(user_to_trade_with)\n\n    # Get the user\'s trading cards\n    user_cards = steam_api.GetTradingCards(user_to_trade_with, bot_trading_card_type)\n\n    # Check if the user has at least 10 trading cards\n    if len(user_cards) >= bot_trading_card_limit:\n        # Send 10 trading cards from the user to the bot\n        steam_api.SendTradingCards(user_to_trade_with, bot_steam_id, user_cards[:bot_trading_card_limit])\n        print(f"Traded with {user_to_trade_with}! Sent {bot_trading_card_limit} trading cards.")\n    else:\n        print(f"{user_to_trade_with} does not have enough trading cards.")\n\n    # Wait for a random amount of time before trading with the next user\n    wait_time = random.randint(60, 3600)  # 1-60 minutes\n    print(f"Waiting {wait_time} seconds before trading with next user...")\n    time.sleep(wait_time)\n```\nPlease note that this code is just an example and you should adjust it according to your specific needs and Steam Trading API limitations. Additionally, make sure to follow Steam\'s trading bot policy and guidelines.\n\nAlso, keep in mind that this code is just an example, you should also consider the following:\n\n* You should check if the user has enough trading cards before sending them, and handle the case where they don\'t.\n* You should handle errors and exceptions properly.\n* You should not send the same card multiple times to the same user.\n* You should not trade with users who have already been traded with in the past.\n* You should not trade with users who have a low trading card count.\n* You should not trade with users who have already sent you all their trading cards.\n\nIt\'s also important to note that Steam has a limit on the number of trading cards that can be sent per day, and you should make sure to stay below that limit to avoid getting your Steam account banned.\n\nI hope this helps! Let me know if you have any questions or need further assistance.',
