      'In TypeScript, you can use the `split()` method to separate the string into an array of strings divided by the slash character, without the need for a regular expression. Here\'s an example:\n\n```typescript\nlet input = "19/20";\nlet result = input.split("/");\n\nlet firstValue = parseInt(result[0]);\nlet secondValue = parseInt(result[1]);\n\nconsole.log(firstValue); // Output: 19\nconsole.log(secondValue); // Output: 20\n```\n\nIn this example, the `split()` method is called on the `input` string with the argument `"/"`. This returns an array of strings, with the first element being the string to the left of the slash and the second element being the string to the right of the slash. The `parseInt()` method is then used to convert the string values to integers.',
