      '```python\ndef cummulative_sum(test_list):\n    """\n    This function takes a list of tuples as input and returns the cumulative sum of all the values present in the tuples.\n\n    Args:\n        test_list (list): A list of tuples, where each tuple contains one or more integers.\n\n    Returns:\n        int: The cumulative sum of all the values present in the tuples.\n    """\n    total_sum = 0\n    for tuple_item in test_list:\n        # Iterate through each tuple in the list\n        for value in tuple_item:\n            # Iterate through each value in the current tuple and add it to the total sum\n            total_sum += value\n    return total_sum\n```\n\nHere\'s how the code works:\n\n1. The function `cummulative_sum` takes a list of tuples `test_list` as input.\n2. We initialize a variable `total_sum` to keep track of the cumulative sum of all the values.\n3. We iterate through each tuple in the `test_list` using a `for` loop.\n4. For each tuple, we iterate through each value in the tuple using another `for` loop.\n5. We add the current value to the `total_sum` variable.\n6. After iterating through all the tuples and their values, we return the final `total_sum`.\n\nThe function will work for the given example outputs:\n\n```python\nprint(cummulative_sum([(1, 3), (5, 6, 7), (2, 6)])) # Output: 30\nprint(cummulative_sum([(2, 4), (6, 7, 8), (3, 7)])) # Output: 37\nprint(cummulative_sum([(3, 5), (7, 8, 9), (4, 8)])) # Output: 44\n```',
